"At some point he was freed, and he began to teach in Rome. Around the year 93 the emperor Domitian, who distrusted philosophers as a source of opposition, expelled them all from Italy. Epictetus crossed the Adriatic and settled at Nicopolis, a port on the western coast of Greece where ships between Rome and the East put in. He opened a school and taught there for some forty years. Young men from the best Roman families came to hear him, and tradition says that the emperor Hadrian visited.": "0440c56a8fca",
"Epictetus asks his students to make an inventory. What do you have that cannot be taken from you? Your property can be confiscated and your body chained. Your reputation is made by other peoples talk. Your family can die. One thing is left, which he calls prohairesis, a word best translated as moral choice or the faculty of choice: the power to judge the impressions that come to you, to accept or refuse them, and to want or not want accordingly. Not even Zeus, he says, can overcome it. A tyrant can say, I will put you in chains, and the answer is, You will chain my leg. My choice not even you can chain.": "83b97711c85a",
"Epictetus was born around the year 50 in Hierapolis, a town in the highlands of what is now western Turkey. His mother was a slave, and so he was one. As a boy he was taken to Rome and became the property of Epaphroditus, who had himself been a slave and had risen to be secretary to the emperor Nero. It was a household at the center of power, and Epictetus watched at close range what men would do for the emperors favor and how quickly it could be lost. His later lectures are full of it: the senator who kisses the hands of a rich freedman, the man who cannot sleep for wondering whether he will be invited to dinner.": "70c7f391483a",
"Everything we have of Epictetus comes through Arrian. The Discourses are lively and uneven. They record informal talks, often prompted by a visitor or by a students question, and they are written in the common Greek of the marketplace and not in the literary Greek that Arrian used for his own books, which is good evidence that he was reporting and not composing. Even so, we have a selection. The formal lectures of the school, on logic and on the texts of earlier Stoics, are not recorded. Half of the books are lost. And the Handbook, by which most readers have known him, is a compression made for a purpose, which keeps the commands and drops the humor, the stories, and most of the warmth. Readers who know only the Handbook often think him harsh. The Discourses show a teacher who teases his students, mocks himself, and loses patience with anyone who wants to discuss philosophy and not to change.": "002004f7d045",
"From this follows his redefinition of freedom and slavery. A free man, as Rome understood the word, was one whom no one could command. Epictetus says that such a man does not exist, since everyone who wants anything outside his own choice has handed power to whoever can give or withhold it. The senator who needs the emperors smile is a slave with a purple border on his toga. The only free person is the one who wants nothing that another can control. The same analysis gives him his account of duty. Each of us has been given roles, as a child, a brother, a citizen, an old man, and the task is to play the role one has well. He does not ask whether the roles are fairly distributed. He asks what a good son would do now.": "20f4fe5e01ff",
"He lived with great simplicity. His house is said to have contained a straw mat, a pallet, and an earthenware lamp, which he used after an iron one was stolen. He remarked that the thief had paid a high price for the lamp, since to get it he had become a thief. He did not marry. Late in life he took in a child whose parents were about to abandon it, and brought a woman into the house to help him raise it.": "89f6cfcb9251",
"He was lame. One ancient account says that the leg was broken by his master, who was twisting it as a punishment. Epictetus is supposed to have said calmly, You will break it, and when it broke, Did I not tell you so? Another source says that he was lame from rheumatism, and the story may have been invented to fit the philosophy. While still a slave he was allowed to attend the lectures of Musonius Rufus, the leading Stoic of the day, who taught that women should study philosophy as men do and that a master has no right to do as he likes with a slave.": "a4565fb647f0",
"Like Socrates, whom he took as his model, Epictetus wrote nothing. That we can read him is due to one student. Arrian of Nicomedia, who later governed a Roman province and wrote the best surviving history of Alexander, attended the school as a young man around 108 and took down what he heard, he says, word for word as far as he could, to preserve for himself a memorial of the teachers thought and frankness of speech. The notes were copied and circulated without his consent, and he then published them as the Discourses, in eight books of which four survive. From these he drew the short Handbook.": "1a6c04f83bd5",
"The Handbook had a long afterlife. Marcus Aurelius thanks a teacher for lending him a copy of Epictetus. Christian monks adapted it as a rule of life, replacing the name of Socrates with that of Paul. It was translated into Chinese by the Jesuit Matteo Ricci in 1605, as The Book of Twenty-Five Sayings, to show Confucian scholars that Western ethics was respectable. And in 1965 a copy went into a prison camp in Hanoi in the memory of an American pilot.": "b02110a8629e",
"The difference lies in what is found in the gap. Epictetus finds a self. Prohairesis is who you really are, it is a portion of the divine reason that orders the world, and the aim of training is to make it sovereign. The Buddha looks in the same place and finds intention, attention, and judgment arising from conditions, with no sovereign among them. Stoic freedom is the independence of a ruler in his citadel. Buddhist freedom is the discovery that there is no citadel and nothing that needs to be defended. Their teachings on feeling for others differ in the same way. Epictetus allows one to groan with a grieving friend, taking care not to groan inwardly, too. The Buddhist ideal is a compassion that is fully felt and does not disturb (Dharma and the West, D20).": "196d0900ce5e",
"The practical psychology is very close. Epictetus teaches his students to meet each impression with the words, Wait for me a little, impression. Let me see what you are. The Buddha teaches the guarding of the sense doors: on seeing a form with the eye, one does not grasp at its signs and features. Both locate freedom in a gap between what arrives and what we do with it, and both hold that the gap can be widened by training. The Stoic word for this watchfulness, prosochē, attention, describes a discipline that Buddhists would recognize as mindfulness, and the second arrow is the Buddhas image for what Epictetus calls adding our view to the thing. Both taught in the open, to anyone, and both distrusted students who collected doctrines without practicing them.": "4724e29e2b43",
"The second doubt is moral. The Stoic retreats to what he controls and declares the rest indifferent. But the rest includes other peoples suffering and the institutions that cause it. Epictetus had been a slave, taught that slaves and senators are equally children of God, and never suggested that slavery should end. The philosopher Isaiah Berlin called this the retreat to the inner citadel, and compared it to a man who cures a wounded leg by cutting it off: I cannot have what I want, so I will teach myself not to want it. Berlin granted that this is a kind of freedom. He denied that it is the kind a person should be content with while the door is still locked from outside.": "a6fa7f5432d0",
"The whole system depends on there being an inner region that the world cannot reach. Critics doubt it from two sides. The first doubt is psychological. Judgment and desire are produced by a brain and a history. A person who has been starved, drugged, or tortured long enough does not keep his power of choice intact, as Stockdale, who admired Epictetus, admitted from experience. If the citadel can be taken, the promise that nothing can harm a good person is false.": "51cb2a3404f7",
